  {
    "question": "State max flow min cut theorem. Find the value of maximal flow in the graph below:",
    "answer": "<p>Here,</p>\n<p>Number assigned to the edges show their capacities. The initial flow is assigned already so saturating it.</p>\n<p><img decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-39331 aligncenter\" src=\"https://i.ibb.co/Lz0WPRpF/60151764f820.png\" alt=\"- Hamro CSIT\" width=\"314\" height=\"153\"></p>\n<p>Augmenting path x → a → b → z</p>\n<p><img fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-39332 aligncenter\" src=\"https://i.ibb.co/gbYGd8SP/03ab32b8d696.png\" alt=\"- Hamro CSIT\" width=\"352\" height=\"182\"></p>\n<p>Augmenting path x → c → d → z</p>\n<p><img decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-39333 aligncenter\" src=\"https://i.ibb.co/20qF0vKM/240ba5dcc89e.png\" alt=\"- Hamro CSIT\" width=\"340\" height=\"185\"></p>\n<p>Augmenting path x → a → d → z</p>\n<p><img decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-39336 aligncenter\" src=\"https://i.ibb.co/YFc06HBf/467df6ebe8c1.png\" alt=\"- Hamro CSIT\" width=\"375\" height=\"203\"></p>\n<p>so,</p>\n<div class=\"table_wrapper\" style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">Path</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">Maximal flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">x → a → b → z</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">x → c → d → z</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">x → a → d → z</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50%; text-align: center;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>we already have initial flow 3 and 6.</p>\n<p>Hence, the maximal flow of the diagram is 18.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n"
