    "question": "Define symmetric closure. What is the symmetric closure of the relation R = {(1,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,3), (3,1), (3,2)} on the set A = {1,2,3}?",
    "answer": "<p>In set theory, the symmetric closure of a binary relation R on a set A is a new binary relation that contains all pairs (a, b) for which (b, a) is already in R. In other words, if (a, b) is in R, then (b, a) is also added to the symmetric closure of R. The symmetric closure makes the relation symmetric, meaning it works in both directions.</p>\n<p>Mathematically, if R is a binary relation on A, then the symmetric closure of R, denoted as R<sup>s</sup>, is defined as:</p>\n<p>R<sup>s</sup> = R ∪ {(b, a) | (a, b) ∈ R}</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<p>R={(1,1) , (1,2) , (2,2) , (2,3) , (3,1) , (3,2)}</p>\n<p>then,</p>\n<p>R<sup>s </sup>= {(1,1) , (1,2) , (1,3) , (2,1) , (2,2) , (2,3) , (3,1) , (3,2) }</p>\n"
  },
